  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.adjacency_spectrum - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.adjacency_spectrum.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.adjacency_spectrum()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the adjacency matrix.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.adjacency_spectrum()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the adjacency matrix.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.adjacency_spectrum()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the adjacency matrix.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the adjacency matrix.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L82521-L82650){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.adjacency_spectrum(\n    which=\"largest\",\n    k=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Adjacency spectrum](../assets/graphs/adjacency-spectrum.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A five-node graph and a bar chart of its five adjacency eigenvalues, sorted from largest to smallest: 2.641, 0.724, -0.589, -1.000, and -1.776. The largest eigenvalue is in the accent color.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns the spectrum (eigenvalues and eigenvectors) of the graph's adjacency matrix. The\nspectrum reveals important structural properties including connectivity, bipartiteness, and\ngraph regularity.\n\nThe adjacency matrix follows the same conventions as NetworkX: edge weights are used when\npresent (otherwise `1.0`), parallel edges sum their weights, and an undirected self-loop\nadds its weight once to the diagonal.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[which]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;largest&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Which eigenvalues to return:\n\n    - `\"all\"`: all `n` eigenvalues\n    - `\"largest\"`: the largest `k` eigenvalues\n    - `\"smallest\"`: the smallest `k` eigenvalues\n\n    For directed graphs, \"largest\" and \"smallest\" are by real part.\n\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Number of eigenvalues to return for `\"largest\"`/`\"smallest\"`. Default is `min(6, n)`\n    where `n` is the number of nodes.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Dictionary with keys:\n\n    - `\"values\"`: list of eigenvalues, sorted descending\n    - `\"vectors\"`: list of unit eigenvectors aligned with `\"values\"`; entry `j` of each\n    vector belongs to node `nodes[j]`\n    - `\"nodes\"`: node IDs, in creation order, giving the order of eigenvector entries\n    - `\"spectral_gap\"`: `λ₁ - λ₂` over the full spectrum (`None` for a single node)\n\n    For directed graphs the adjacency matrix is not symmetric, so `\"values\"` holds Python\n    `complex` numbers sorted by real part, and `\"vectors\"` and `\"spectral_gap\"` are `None`.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is empty, `which` is invalid, or the eigenvalue computation fails to\n    converge.\n:::\n\n## Common Use Cases {.doc-common-use-cases}\n\n- spectral clustering: community detection via eigenvectors\n- graph embedding: represent nodes using eigenvector coordinates\n- bipartite detection: check if spectrum is symmetric about `0`\n- random walk analysis: eigenvalues determine mixing time\n- graph comparison: compare spectral distributions\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe adjacency spectrum captures a graph's global structure in a bounded list of\nreal numbers, and many core properties are recoverable directly from it. The\n*spectral radius* $\\rho(A) = \\max_i |\\lambda_i|$ satisfies $\\rho(A) \\le \\Delta(G)$\nand hits that bound exactly for regular graphs, where every eigenvalue is bounded\nabove by the shared degree. The *spectral gap* $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2$ quantifies\nexpansion, and a zero gap witnesses disconnection. Bipartiteness reveals itself as\nspectrum symmetry about the origin, since a graph is bipartite iff its adjacency\nspectrum equals its negation as a multiset. Signature spectra include\n$K_n \\to \\{n - 1, -1, \\ldots, -1\\}$, the cycle\n$C_n \\to \\{2\\cos(2\\pi k / n)\\}_{k = 0}^{n - 1}$, and the star\n$S_n \\to \\{\\sqrt{n - 1}, 0, \\ldots, 0, -\\sqrt{n - 1}\\}$.\n\nFor undirected graphs with at least 400 nodes, `\"largest\"` and `\"smallest\"` with a\nsmall `k` use a sparse iterative solver (Chebyshev-filtered subspace iteration) that\ntouches only the graph's edges, so they scale to graphs with hundreds of thousands of\nnodes (a few seconds for 200,000 nodes). It is a block method, so it resolves repeated\neigenvalues, which are common in graph spectra. Results match the dense solver to about\n`1e-11` relative to the spectral radius. Convergence slows when the requested\neigenvalues are tightly clustered relative to the spread of the whole spectrum.\n\nEverything else runs a dense $O(n^3)$ eigendecomposition: `\"all\"`, small graphs, and\ndirected graphs (Francis double-shift QR). This is practical up to a few thousand\nnodes. Eigenvector signs are normalized so that the first non-negligible entry is\npositive, making results reproducible.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~adjacency_matrix`) \n\n:   The matrix whose eigenvalues these are.\n\n[](`~laplacian_spectrum`) \n\n:   The eigenvalues of the Laplacian instead.\n\n[](`~modularity_spectrum`) \n\n:   The eigenvalues of the modularity matrix.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe complete graph on five nodes has eigenvalues `4` and four times `-1`:\n\n::: {#bb9486c8 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport itertools\n\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ncomplete = ec.Graph.from_edges(list(itertools.combinations(\"ABCDE\", 2)))\nvalues = complete.adjacency_spectrum(which=\"all\")[\"values\"]\n\n[round(v, 3) + 0.0 for v in values]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[4.0, -1.0, -1.0, -1.0, -1.0]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A mirrored spectrum\n\n`teams()` links the 8 workers of a made-up software team to the 8 tasks of a release that\neach can do, weighted by the hours it would take them. Every link joins a worker to a\ntask, so the graph is bipartite, and its spectrum is the same read from either end, with\nthe signs flipped:\n\n::: {#0899d080 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nteam = ec.teams()\nvalues = team.adjacency_spectrum(which=\"all\")[\"values\"]\n\n[round(v, 2) + 0.0 for v in values]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[18.52,\n 14.87,\n 9.97,\n 8.42,\n 6.02,\n 1.89,\n 1.3,\n 0.0,\n 0.0,\n -1.3,\n -1.89,\n -6.02,\n -8.42,\n -9.97,\n -14.87,\n -18.52]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#4dda45eb .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n(\n    team.layout_bipartite(align=\"horizontal\", aspect_ratio=0.4)\n    .plot(layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", height=300)\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://